From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: anxelok <anxelok@localhost>
Date: Fri, 3 Jul 2026 11:49:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Do not latch plugins to REFUSED on service call cycles

A detected service call cycle threw to break the recursion but also
recorded a persistent refusal, which escalated the provider plugin's
compatibility mode to REFUSED. That mode is sticky and plugin-wide, so
a single cycle silently dropped every future event dispatch for that
plugin on every thread. This was the only remaining path that could
push a plugin into REFUSED.

Keep the recursion-breaking throw but record the cycle as a transient
refusal, matching how apartment contention is handled: the dispatch at
hand fails, diagnostics stay visible, and the plugin is re-evaluated
normally on the next call.

diff --git a/src/main/java/io/papermc/paper/plugin/manager/compat/CompatibilityServicesManager.java b/src/main/java/io/papermc/paper/plugin/manager/compat/CompatibilityServicesManager.java
index cea50b424187f9a6003cfc798a5e16a32e8f08f4..282070319db8bfbb15ad37028b2e490e5608efe0 100644
--- a/src/main/java/io/papermc/paper/plugin/manager/compat/CompatibilityServicesManager.java
+++ b/src/main/java/io/papermc/paper/plugin/manager/compat/CompatibilityServicesManager.java
@@ -159,7 +159,12 @@ public final class CompatibilityServicesManager implements ServicesManager {
 
             final PluginCompatibilityProfile profile = compatibilityKernel.profileFor(this.providerPlugin);
             if (graphEntry.cycleDetected()) {
-                compatibilityKernel.diagnostics().recordRefusal(profile, "service cycle " + graphEntry.cyclePath());
+                // Loom Fix 0 - a service call cycle is a PER-CALL condition: the throw below already breaks
+                // the recursion for this one invocation. It must not latch the provider's persistent mode to
+                // REFUSED, which silently dropped every future event dispatch for that plugin on every
+                // thread. Record it transiently so the cycle stays visible in diagnostics without poisoning
+                // the plugin.
+                compatibilityKernel.diagnostics().recordTransientRefusal(profile, "service cycle " + graphEntry.cyclePath());
                 callGraph.exit();
                 throw new IllegalStateException("Compatibility service cycle detected: " + graphEntry.cyclePath());
             }
